WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.720
Vacuum stills at 50 to 100 millimeters of mercury made lubricant cuts without cracking the oil. This is First Principles Daily, episode 167. Before the 1920s, lubricant fractions came from batch stills that heated heavy petroleum residues at atmospheric pressure until the oil cracked into lighter, less valuable products. Engineers at the US Bureau of Mines and in private labs then built tall columns that lowered the boiling point by pulling a vacuum.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.420
So, the same residues could yield clean base oil cuts while the temperature stayed below

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:32.460
the cracking threshold.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:38.300
Repeated heating cycles gave way to a continuous flow through a single vessel, and the steel shell

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.680
and the vacuum pumps became the dominant hardware.

00:00:41.702 --> 00:00:46.302
Atmospheric batch stills had been the standard since the earliest refineries because operators

00:00:46.302 --> 00:00:50.102
simply scaled up the equipment already used for kerosene and fuel oil.

00:00:50.682 --> 00:00:53.002
Each batch required repeated heating and cooling.

00:00:53.002 --> 00:00:57.922
The heavy residues had to reach temperatures high enough to drive off the lubricant molecules,

00:00:58.302 --> 00:01:02.422
and those temperatures often exceeded the thermal stability limit of the oil itself.

00:01:03.042 --> 00:01:08.862
In 1926, BT Brooks, writing an industrial and engineering chemistry, described how those

00:01:08.862 --> 00:01:13.502
conditions produced cracked, unstable fractions that needed extra treating steps later.

00:01:14.122 --> 00:01:18.682
Refiner's accepted the losses because the apparatus already existed, and the market

00:01:18.682 --> 00:01:23.602
for lubricants was small enough that incremental yield improvements did not justify new capital.

00:01:24.322 --> 00:01:29.482
The finished cost therefore embedded the price of loss material, extra labor for batch handling

00:01:29.482 --> 00:01:35.182
and the downstream processing required to stabilize cracked oil. No one had measured a raw material

00:01:35.182 --> 00:01:40.542
floor because the process itself was treated as fixed. The convention persisted because every

00:01:40.542 --> 00:01:46.102
new refinery copied the layout of the previous one, adding only incremental capacity rather than

00:01:46.102 --> 00:01:48.482
questioning the pressure at which the separation occurred.

00:01:49.022 --> 00:01:54.002
When a batch finished, the still had to be cooled, emptied, cleaned, and recharged, which

00:01:54.002 --> 00:01:56.902
multiplied the fuel burned per barrel of finished base stock.

00:01:57.482 --> 00:02:01.882
The cracked material then required additional clay treating or acid washing that atmospheric

00:02:01.882 --> 00:02:03.402
distillation never avoided.

00:02:04.002 --> 00:02:08.682
Those extra vessels and their operating crews became accepted overhead, because no one

00:02:08.682 --> 00:02:13.142
had yet demonstrated that a different pressure regime could eliminate the cracking step entirely.

00:02:13.142 --> 00:02:18.341
The vacuum approach began with the physical fact that boiling point drops when surrounding

00:02:18.341 --> 00:02:19.181
pressure falls.

00:02:19.401 --> 00:02:29.721
The 1930 Bureau of Mines report BM-RI-26 and the 1931 patent US 1804941 are where that

00:02:29.721 --> 00:02:34.121
operating band is written down and both show distillation temperatures remaining under

00:02:34.121 --> 00:02:36.001
425 degrees Celsius.

00:02:36.581 --> 00:02:41.881
The sources list the main physical inputs as the steel shell of the column, the steam ejectors

00:02:41.881 --> 00:02:46.741
or vacuum pumps that maintain the low pressure, and the reboiler heat needed to vaporize

00:02:46.741 --> 00:02:47.241
the feed.

00:02:47.741 --> 00:02:52.981
No commodity prices for those items appear in the reports, so a precise magic wand number

00:02:52.981 --> 00:02:54.861
cannot be assembled from the given data.

00:02:55.501 --> 00:02:58.481
The magic wand number would be those inputs added together.

00:02:59.021 --> 00:03:03.601
The steel, plus the energy to sustain the vacuum, plus the energy to reboil.

00:03:04.061 --> 00:03:08.201
Without prices, that sum stays a description of the floor, not a dollar figure.

00:03:08.201 --> 00:03:12.721
year. The first principles ratio would be the finish price of the lubricant base stock

00:03:12.721 --> 00:03:18.201
divided by that floor. The finish price is absent from the sources too, so the division

00:03:18.201 --> 00:03:22.101
has no second number and the ratio cannot be stated as a single result.

00:03:22.761 --> 00:03:28.441
What the documents do show is the sequence of design changes that moved cost downward. Continuous

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:33.981
feed replaced batch charging. Side stream draws collected light, medium and heavy lube cuts

00:03:33.981 --> 00:03:39.461
in one pass. Steam stripping inside the tower removed the last traces of volatile material

00:03:39.461 --> 00:03:44.801
without extra vessels. Each of those steps eliminated a separate heating cycle and the fuel and

00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:52.041
labor that went with it. The 1938 National Bureau of Standards paper RP-1142 records one

00:03:52.041 --> 00:03:58.121
concrete run that processed 240 gallons of duaxed Oklahoma City crude at pressures down to 1

00:03:58.121 --> 00:04:03.241
millimeter of mercury, confirming that narrow stable fractions could be taken without cracking.

00:04:03.941 --> 00:04:07.961
The key engineering trade-off was keeping the column diameter large enough for the vapor

00:04:07.961 --> 00:04:11.201
volume at low pressure while still maintaining the vacuum.

00:04:11.941 --> 00:04:16.021
The sources note that diameters increased compared with atmospheric towers precisely

00:04:16.021 --> 00:04:17.401
to manage that volume.

00:04:18.001 --> 00:04:23.201
A larger diameter means more steel plate per unit height, yet the single-tall vessel still

00:04:23.201 --> 00:04:28.001
replace the multiple smaller stills and they're connecting piping that batch operations required.

00:04:28.581 --> 00:04:33.321
Continuous flow also removed the need for intermediate storage tanks between heating stages,

00:04:33.801 --> 00:04:37.061
cutting both the steel inventory and the heat loss during transfers.

00:04:37.821 --> 00:04:42.741
Operators had to solve entrainment, where liquid droplets could be carried upward into the wrong

00:04:42.741 --> 00:04:48.521
side stream. The sources describe internal baffles and wash zones that captured those droplets without

00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:53.761
adding an entire second column, maintaining the vacuum demanded reliable steam ejectors

00:04:53.761 --> 00:04:59.341
size for the non-condensable gases that inevitably entered with the feed. Any leak raised the

00:04:59.341 --> 00:05:04.761
pressure and risk cracking the heaviest fractions. The redesign therefore traded higher capital

00:05:04.761 --> 00:05:09.701
in the column shell and ejector system for lower operating energy per barrel once the unit

00:05:09.701 --> 00:05:15.881
reached steady state. Brooks in that 1926 paper already noted that the lower temperature preserve

00:05:15.881 --> 00:05:21.361
the viscosity index of the base oil, which reduced the volume of downstream additives needed to

00:05:21.361 --> 00:05:27.622
meet finished lubricant specifications. Yields of usable base oil rose because less material was

00:05:27.622 --> 00:05:35.382
lost to cracking, and the resulting oils required fewer finishing steps. The 1927-1938 reports treat

00:05:35.382 --> 00:05:40.782
the continuous vacuum tower as the new baseline rather than an experiment, indicating that the

00:05:40.782 --> 00:05:45.642
capital cost of the taller column and vacuum system was recovered through higher throughput and

00:05:45.642 --> 00:05:51.202
better product quality. The gap that remains is the energy still required to maintain the vacuum

00:05:51.202 --> 00:05:56.762
and the steel and fabrication cost of the column itself. Those elements sit close to the physical

00:05:56.762 --> 00:06:02.182
floor once the process is running at scale. What the sources do not resolve is how much of the

00:06:02.182 --> 00:06:06.982
final lubricant price is set by crude oil acquisition rather than by the distillation step.

00:06:07.602 --> 00:06:12.622
That share is a feedstock price, not a column problem, and it should not be dressed up as one.

00:06:12.622 --> 00:06:17.762
The larger vapor volume at low pressure also meant that any given mass of feed occupied

00:06:17.762 --> 00:06:22.802
more space inside the column, so throughput gains depended on both the increased diameter

00:06:22.802 --> 00:06:24.422
and continuous operation.

00:06:25.062 --> 00:06:29.882
Once the vacuum system was running, the marginal cost of an additional barrel became dominated

00:06:29.882 --> 00:06:34.542
by the reboiler heat and the steam supplied to the ejectors rather than by additional

00:06:34.542 --> 00:06:35.962
labor or loss product.

00:06:36.602 --> 00:06:41.402
A tall column whose diameter is size for low pressure vapor flow shows how one change

00:06:41.402 --> 00:06:45.222
in surrounding pressure removes an entire chain of thermal damage and rework.

00:06:45.962 --> 00:06:47.922
Cracking was not the aim of the old still.

00:06:48.622 --> 00:06:53.382
Atmospheric pressure set a boiling temperature the residue could not survive, so the equipment

00:06:53.382 --> 00:06:55.942
not the oil's stability limit dictated the heat.

00:06:56.522 --> 00:07:02.142
Every refinery that copied the last layout copied that dictate and then paid in loss material

00:07:02.142 --> 00:07:05.502
and later repair for a temperature the process never needed.

00:07:06.042 --> 00:07:10.682
The vacuum move asked which surrounding condition was forcing the temperature and answered

00:07:10.682 --> 00:07:15.422
with pressure. Hold pressure low enough, and the heavy molecules can leave as vapor while

00:07:15.422 --> 00:07:20.602
the liquid stays intact. The wider shell is the one-time cost of giving that vapor a path.

00:07:21.182 --> 00:07:26.002
Spent in a single vessel instead of in a family of stills. Once the unit is at scale,

00:07:26.262 --> 00:07:30.722
the energy to hold the vacuum and the steel and fabrication of the column sit close to the

00:07:30.722 --> 00:07:39.106
physical floor. The share of a finished lubricant's price that is simply crude acquisition is a

00:07:39.106 --> 00:07:45.166
distillation step. Name that gap as feedstock, not as an unsolved column. The same logic

00:07:45.166 --> 00:07:49.866
applies whenever a process temperature is dictated by the equipment rather than by the stability

00:07:49.866 --> 00:07:55.206
limit of the material being handled. Analogy will keep the temperature the last machine required.

00:07:55.786 --> 00:08:00.346
A front scratch pass looks for the surrounding condition that forces it, and asks whether

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:05.226
changing that condition deletes the rework rather than polishing it. Who will next measure

00:08:05.226 --> 00:08:10.346
the energy actually consumed inside an operating vacuum tower and ask whether a different internal

00:08:10.346 --> 00:08:12.266
geometry could cut that figure further.

00:08:12.786 --> 00:08:15.266
That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown.

00:08:15.986 --> 00:08:18.306
The ideas travel further when you pass them on.

00:08:18.766 --> 00:08:21.506
Send this to a fellow builder, I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

